      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2010 American College of Allergy, Asthma & Immunology. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Nerve growth factor (NGF), combined with its high-affinity receptor tyrosine kinase receptor A (TrkA), has been reported to be involved in the pathogenesis of asthma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To investigate whether the downstream protein ankyrin-rich membrane spanning (ARMS), a novel transmembrane substrate of protein kinase D (Kidins220), is activated in the pathogenesis of asthma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "The asthmatic model was established by the inhalation of ovalbumin in BALB/c mice. The effects of NGF and TrkA on Kidins220/ARMS in an allergic airway challenge were assessed by administering anti-NGF or anti-TrkA antibody to the mice. Pathologic changes in the bronchi and lung tissues were examined by means of hematoxylin and eosin staining; the inflammatory cells in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid (BALF) were counted; and co-expression of ARMS and TrkA in BALF cells was observed by means of immunofluorescence. In addition, Kidins220/ARMS, CrkL, NGF, TrkA protein, and Kidins220 messenger RNA levels were determined using Western blot or quantitative reverse transcription-polymerase chain reaction."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Using fluorescence microscopy, we found that Kidins220 and TrkA were co-expressed on the membranes of the BALF cells of asthmatic mice. Compared with expression in control animals, Kidins220/ARMS, CrkL, NGF, and TrkA were overexpressed in the lungs after allergen challenge. Moreover, after the mice were treated with anti-NGF or anti-TrkA, the Kidins220/ARMS levels and allergen-induced airway inflammation decreased."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "These results suggest that Kidins220/ARMS partly participates in the pathogenesis of asthma through the NGF-TrkA signaling pathway, possibly representing a new mechanism in asthma."
          }
        ]
      },
